Chapter: ReflectRender
  cites Reflect chapter ReflectModel
  cites Terrain chapter TerrainModel
  cites Water chapter OceanModel
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A lake among the terrain of wave 3, reflecting it by screen-space
 reflection. Two passes over the same frame, which is the shape every SSR
 implementation has:

   Pass 1 draws the scene normally and, as it goes, records for each pixel
   the distance to what it drew and that surface's normal. Colour goes to
   the framebuffer, the rest to the G-buffer.
   Pass 2 walks back over the water pixels only. For each it reconstructs
   the world point, reflects the view direction about the stored normal,
   and marches that reflected ray against the depth buffer pass 1 just
   wrote. Where it crosses a surface, the colour already in the
   framebuffer at that pixel is the reflected colour.

 Pass 2 cannot run until pass 1 has finished the whole frame, and that is
 not an implementation detail: a reflection can land on a pixel far from
 the one being shaded, so any pixel of the image may be needed. Interleaving
 the passes would sample a half-drawn frame, which is the same failure the
 magenta clear was added to catch and would be much harder to see.

Section: The Scene

 The water line. The field runs from 33 to 207 metres with a mean of 119,
 measured over a 60x60 grid rather than guessed, and 108 puts roughly the
 lower third under water: a lake with a shoreline, not a puddle and not a
 flood.

  rr-lake-y : Real
  rr-lake-y = 108.0

  rr-max-dist : Real
  rr-max-dist = 5000.0

  rr-max-step : Real
  rr-max-step = 24.0

  rr-first-step : Real
  rr-first-step = 6.0

  rr-step-growth : Real
  rr-step-growth = 1.035

  rr-march-steps : Integer
  rr-march-steps = 260

  rr-refine-iters : Integer
  rr-refine-iters = 12

 The height-field march, again. `TerrainRender` has one and it cannot be
 cited, because it carries an `opening`; but this one is not the same
 traversal anyway. It has to resolve a lake plane against the ground and
 decide which of the two the ray met first, and the step cap is here for
 the reason recorded in that chapter -- the bracket must not hold two
 crossings.

  rr-under : V3, V3, Real -> Boolean
  rr-under (origin) (dir) (t) =
    let p = v-add origin (v-scale dir t)
    in p.y < t-height (p.x) (p.z)

  rr-refine : V3, V3, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  rr-refine (origin) (dir) (lo) (hi) (iters) =
    if iters <= 0 then hi
    else let mid = (lo + hi) * 0.5
    in if rr-under origin dir mid then rr-refine origin dir lo mid (iters - 1)
    else rr-refine origin dir mid hi (iters - 1)

  rr-ground : V3, V3, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  rr-ground (origin) (dir) (t) (step) (steps) =
    if steps <= 0 then 0.0 - 1.0
    else if t > rr-max-dist then 0.0 - 1.0
    else let t2 = t + step
    in if rr-under origin dir t2 then rr-refine origin dir t t2 rr-refine-iters
    else let grown = step * rr-step-growth
    in let next = if grown > rr-max-step then rr-max-step else grown
    in rr-ground origin dir t2 next (steps - 1)

 Where the ray meets the lake surface, if it does. A ray climbing away
 from the water never meets it.

  rr-lake-t : V3, V3 -> Real
  rr-lake-t (origin) (dir) =
    if dir.y > (0.0 - 0.000001) then 0.0 - 1.0
    else let t = (rr-lake-y - origin.y) / dir.y
    in if t < 0.0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else t

  Scene = record { dist : Real, water : Boolean, found : Boolean }

 Nearest wins, with one guard: the lake only exists where the ground under
 it is lower than the water. On a scene this shape the guard is redundant,
 because ground standing above the lake plane would have stopped the ray
 before it got there, but it costs one height sample and it makes the rule
 explicit rather than a consequence.

  rr-scene : V3, V3 -> Scene
  rr-scene (origin) (dir) =
    let tg = rr-ground origin dir 0.0 rr-first-step rr-march-steps
    in let tl = rr-lake-t origin dir
    in if tl < 0.0 then
      (if tg < 0.0 then Scene { dist = 0.0, water = False, found = False }
       else Scene { dist = tg, water = False, found = True })
    else let lp = v-add origin (v-scale dir tl)
    in let wet = t-height (lp.x) (lp.z) < rr-lake-y
    in if wet == False then
      (if tg < 0.0 then Scene { dist = 0.0, water = False, found = False }
       else Scene { dist = tg, water = False, found = True })
    else if tg < 0.0 then Scene { dist = tl, water = True, found = True }
    else if tl < tg then Scene { dist = tl, water = True, found = True }
    else Scene { dist = tg, water = False, found = True }

 The water normal. Two crossed ripples, small enough to keep the surface
 readable as water rather than as a disturbed mirror. This is what makes
 the reflection wobble, and it is also what the roughness perturbation in
 the model acts on top of.

  rr-ripple-scale : Real
  rr-ripple-scale = 0.055

 The ripple amplitude is small, and it has to be. A normal tilted by a is
 a reflected ray turned by 2a, and at this viewpoint the reflected ray
 clears the horizon by only a few degrees; a slope of 0.03 turns it by
 more than three, which is enough to swing each pixel between striking the
 far ridge and escaping into the sky. The reflection then arrives as
 salt-and-pepper rather than an image. Real water does break up a distant
 reflection exactly this way, but the amplitude that reads as water at a
 steep angle is far too much at a grazing one.

  rr-ripple-amp : Real
  rr-ripple-amp = 0.0075

  rr-water-normal : V3 -> V3
  rr-water-normal (p) =
    let a = w-sin (p.x * rr-ripple-scale) * w-cos (p.z * rr-ripple-scale * 0.8)
    in let b = w-sin ((p.x + p.z) * rr-ripple-scale * 0.61)
    in v-norm (V3 { x = a * rr-ripple-amp, y = 1.0, z = b * rr-ripple-amp })

Section: Sky and Palette

  rr-sky : V3 -> V3
  rr-sky (dir) =
    let t = w-sat (dir.y * 2.0 + 0.08)
    in let base = v-lerp rr-horizon-color terrain-sky-color t
    in let s = w-sat (v-dot dir (v-norm terrain-sun))
    in let disc = w-powi s 64
    in let halo = w-powi s 8 * 0.20
    in v-add base (v-scale terrain-sun-color (disc + halo))

  rr-horizon-color : V3
  rr-horizon-color = V3 { x = 0.74, y = 0.80, z = 0.86 }

  rr-water-deep : V3
  rr-water-deep = V3 { x = 0.020, y = 0.055, z = 0.075 }

  rr-water-f0 : Real
  rr-water-f0 = 0.02

  rr-water-rough : Real
  rr-water-rough = 0.055

  rr-fog-start : Real
  rr-fog-start = 500.0

  rr-fog-end : Real
  rr-fog-end = 4600.0

  rr-fog : V3, V3, Real -> V3
  rr-fog (color) (dir) (dist) =
    let f = w-sat ((dist - rr-fog-start) / (rr-fog-end - rr-fog-start))
    in v-lerp color (rr-sky dir) (f * f)

Section: Framebuffer

  rr-fb-base : Integer
  rr-fb-base = #BF000000

  rr-width : Integer
  rr-width = 640

  rr-height : Integer
  rr-height = 400

  rr-pack : Real, Real, Real -> Integer
  rr-pack (r) (g) (b) =
    let ri = real-to-int (w-sat r * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let gi = real-to-int (w-sat g * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let bi = real-to-int (w-sat b * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in ri * 65536 + gi * 256 + bi

  rr-unpack : Integer -> V3
  rr-unpack (c) =
    let b = int-mod c 256
    in let g = int-mod (c / 256) 256
    in let r = int-mod (c / 65536) 256
    in V3 { x = real-from-int r / 255.0, y = real-from-int g / 255.0, z = real-from-int b / 255.0 }

  rr-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-put (x) (y) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= rr-width then 0
    else if y >= rr-height then 0
    else poke-32 rr-fb-base ((y * rr-width + x) * 4) color

 A traced sample fills a 2x2 block, so reading the colour of traced pixel
 (tx,ty) means reading the framebuffer at twice those coordinates. Pass 2
 samples through here, which is the moment the technique becomes literal:
 this reads the picture it is drawing.

  rr-get-traced : Integer, Integer -> V3
  rr-get-traced (tx) (ty) =
    rr-unpack (peek-32 rr-fb-base ((ty * 2 * rr-width + tx * 2) * 4))

  rr-put-block : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-put-block (tx) (ty) (color) =
    let x = tx * 2
    in let y = ty * 2
    in let a = rr-put x y color
    in let b = rr-put (x + 1) y color
    in let c = rr-put x (y + 1) color
    in rr-put (x + 1) (y + 1) color

  rr-clear-color : Integer
  rr-clear-color = #FF00FF

  rr-clear-row : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-clear-row (x) (y) =
    if x >= rr-width then 0
    else let d = rr-put x y rr-clear-color
    in rr-clear-row (x + 1) y

  rr-clear : Integer -> Integer
  rr-clear (y) =
    if y >= rr-height then 0
    else let d = rr-clear-row 0 y
    in rr-clear (y + 1)

Section: Pass One

 Draw the scene, and record what was drawn. The water is shaded here
 WITHOUT its reflection: deep water colour plus the sun's specular
 highlight. Pass 2 adds the reflection on top, which is why this pass can
 be a normal render and why the technique bolts onto an existing one.

  rr-shade-water : V3, V3, V3 -> V3
  rr-shade-water (p) (dir) (n) =
    let l = v-norm terrain-sun
    in let spec = ggx-spec n (v-neg dir) l rr-water-rough rr-water-f0
    in let sky-tint = v-scale (rr-sky (rf-reflect dir n)) 0.10
    in v-add (v-add rr-water-deep sky-tint) (v-scale terrain-sun-color (spec * 26.0))

  rr-pass1-px : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass1-px (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let dir = rf-ray cam tx ty
    in let sc = rr-scene (cam.eye) dir
    in if sc.found == False then
      (let s = rr-sky dir
       in let a = gb-store-depth tx ty (0.0 - 1.0)
       in rr-put-block tx ty (rr-pack (s.x) (s.y) (s.z)))
    else let p = v-add (cam.eye) (v-scale dir (sc.dist))
    in let vz = sc.dist * v-dot dir (cam.cf)
    in if sc.water then
      (let n = rr-water-normal p
       in let c = rr-shade-water p dir n
       in let a = gb-store-depth tx ty vz
       in let b = gb-store-normal tx ty n 1
       in rr-put-block tx ty (rr-pack (c.x) (c.y) (c.z)))
    else let n = t-normal (p.x) (p.z)
    in let lit = t-shade p n
    in let c = rr-fog lit dir (sc.dist)
    in let a = gb-store-depth tx ty vz
    in let b = gb-store-normal tx ty n 0
    in rr-put-block tx ty (rr-pack (c.x) (c.y) (c.z))

  rr-pass1-row : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass1-row (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    if tx >= gb-width then 0
    else let c = rr-pass1-px cam tx ty
    in rr-pass1-row cam (tx + 1) ty

  rr-pass1 : Cam, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass1 (cam) (ty) =
    if ty >= gb-height then 0
    else let d = rr-pass1-row cam 0 ty
    in rr-pass1 cam (ty + 1)

Section: Pass Two

 The reflection. Everything that makes SSR what it is happens in these
 twenty lines.

 The march starts a little way off the surface along the normal. Starting
 exactly on it means the first probe compares the surface against its own
 recorded depth and reports a hit immediately, and the water reflects
 itself -- a flat wash of water colour that looks almost plausible, which
 is what makes it worth naming.

 On a miss the sky stands in. A real implementation would fall back to a
 pre-rendered environment probe, and the sky IS that probe here; the fade
 toward the screen edge blends between them so the boundary does not draw
 a line across the water.

  rr-start-offset : Real
  rr-start-offset = 0.35

  rr-pass2-px : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass2-px (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    if gb-load-flag tx ty /= 1 then 0
    else let d = gb-load-depth tx ty
    in if d < 0.0 then 0
    else let dir = rf-ray cam tx ty
    in let n = gb-load-normal tx ty
    in let along = v-dot dir (cam.cf)
    in let p = v-add (cam.eye) (v-scale dir (d / along))
    in let mirror = rf-reflect dir n
    in let j1 = c-jitter-like tx ty
    in let j2 = c-jitter-like ty tx
    in let r = rf-perturb mirror rr-water-rough j1 j2
    in let origin = v-add p (v-scale n rr-start-offset)
    in let h = rf-trace cam origin r 1
    in let sky = rr-sky r
    in let refl = if h.found then v-lerp sky (rr-get-traced (h.hx) (h.hy)) (h.fade) else sky
    in let w = rf-weight n dir rr-water-f0
    in let base = rr-get-traced tx ty
    in let final = v-add base (v-scale refl w)
    in let d2 = rr-put-block tx ty (rr-pack (final.x) (final.y) (final.z))
    in if h.found then 1 else 0

 The hit count is ACCUMULATED and not recorded in the G-buffer, and the
 first version of this got that wrong in a way worth keeping a note about.
 Marking each pixel's outcome back into the flag byte seemed a tidy way to
 count them, but pass 2 READS that same byte: every water pixel already
 processed carried its outcome instead of its material, so the
 self-reflection reject could never match and the buffer the march depends
 on was being rewritten underneath it, row by row, as the pass advanced.
 A pass that consumes a buffer must not also write it.

  rr-pass2-row : Cam, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass2-row (cam) (tx) (ty) (acc) =
    if tx >= gb-width then acc
    else rr-pass2-row cam (tx + 1) ty (acc + rr-pass2-px cam tx ty)

  rr-pass2 : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-pass2 (cam) (ty) (acc) =
    if ty >= gb-height then acc
    else rr-pass2 cam (ty + 1) (rr-pass2-row cam 0 ty acc)

 A cheap decorrelated value per pixel, the same shape as the cloud model's
 jitter and for the same reason: neighbouring pixels must not perturb their
 reflection the same way, or the noise organises itself into bands.

  c-jitter-like : Integer, Integer -> Real
  c-jitter-like (x) (y) =
    let h0 = bit-and (x * 73856093 + y * 19349663 + 83492791) 2147483647
    in let h1 = bit-and (h0 * 1103515245 + 12345) 2147483647
    in let h2 = bit-and (h1 * 1103515245 + 12345) 2147483647
    in real-from-int h2 * 0.000000000465661287308

Section: Counting

 How much water is in frame, and how much of it found something to
 reflect. Both are printed, because a reflection pass that runs over zero
 water pixels and one that runs over thousands and misses every time
 produce the same image, and neither announces itself.

  rr-count-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-count-row (tx) (ty) (want) (acc) =
    if tx >= gb-width then acc
    else let f = gb-load-flag tx ty
    in let hit = if f == want then 1 else 0
    in rr-count-row (tx + 1) ty want (acc + hit)

  rr-count : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-count (ty) (want) (acc) =
    if ty >= gb-height then acc
    else rr-count (ty + 1) want (rr-count-row 0 ty want acc)

Section: Holding the Frame

  rr-tick-addr : Integer
  rr-tick-addr = 28672

  rr-hold-ticks : Integer
  rr-hold-ticks = 220

  rr-hold-fuel : Integer
  rr-hold-fuel = 40000000000

  rr-hold : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rr-hold (until) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if peek-32 rr-tick-addr 0 >= until then 1
    else rr-hold until (fuel - 1)

  rr-hold-frame : Integer
  rr-hold-frame = rr-hold (peek-32 rr-tick-addr 0 + rr-hold-ticks) rr-hold-fuel

Section: Driver

 Placed over the basin the height scan found around z = 1800, high enough
 to stand clear of ground that reaches 142 at z = 900, and pitched down
 just enough to put the far shore and the mountains behind it in frame.
 A reflective surface is only worth rendering if there is something above
 it to reflect, and on screen for the reflection to sample.

 Low over the water, and that is a physical decision rather than a
 compositional one. Fresnel governs how much a dielectric reflects, and
 for water it runs from about 0.02 head-on to nearly 1.0 at grazing
 incidence. A camera looking DOWN at a lake therefore sees almost no
 reflection, correctly: the first framing here sat 80 m above the surface
 and produced water that was accurately, uselessly dark. Dropping the eye
 to a few metres above the waterline is what puts the reflection where it
 can be seen, and it is also what a photographer would do.

  rr-eye : V3
  rr-eye = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 124.0, z = 1650.0 }

  rr-forward : V3
  rr-forward = V3 { x = 0.05, y = 0.0 - 0.045, z = 0.998 }

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "Reflection render (screen-space, GGX weighted, two passes)"
    print-line-uni ("resolution = " & show rr-width & "x" & show rr-height & ", traced at " & show gb-width & "x" & show gb-height)
    print-line-uni ("lake y = " & show rr-lake-y & ", roughness = " & show rr-water-rough & ", f0 = " & show rr-water-f0)
    print-line-uni ("ssr steps = " & show rf-steps & ", thickness = " & show rf-thickness)
    let cam = rf-camera rr-eye rr-forward
    in let c0 = rr-clear 0
    in let g0 = gb-clear 0
    in let p1 = rr-pass1 cam 0
    in act
      let water = rr-count 0 1 0
      in let hits = rr-pass2 cam 0 0
      in act
        print-line-uni ("water pixels = " & show water)
        print-line-uni ("ssr hit = " & show hits)
        print-line-uni ("ssr miss = " & show (water - hits))
        print-line-uni ("held = " & show rr-hold-frame)
      end
    end
  end
